g epic builds against its quota/semantics contract and transitively\nenables HLR-1, even though E002's only direct HLR (HLR-3) is P1 — see Health\nWarnings. E004 is a `docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance;\nits P1 derives from the strategy frontmatter priority `Major` (Major → P1).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: metering schema contract\"] --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard chargeback reporting\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Quota reference + attribution semantics\"] --> E003\n    E002 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\nRoots (no dependencies): E001, E002. Critical path: E001 → E003 → E004\n(length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's open question — the metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands — determines what the reporting implementation builds against. The investigation produces the reporting-side data contract; until it resolves, the reporting epic's approach is unknown. True gate: `rewrite` — if the finalized schema does not support per-project GPU-hour aggregation, the reporting implementation is rewritten against the actual schema (or extensions are requested to the metering strategy). |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact another epic builds against) | E002 delivers the validated per-project Kueue quota reference and GPU-hour attribution semantics; the Dashboard reporting view's code builds against that contract to render consumption-vs-quota comparison. Not a configuration reference — the quota mapping and semantics define the data shape the view consumes. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | The documentation epic must be blocked by every Implementation epic; it also derives its report-semantics content from the attribution semantics E002 defines. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes what was built — the reporting view, export, and API — so it lands last. |\n\nNo edges between E001 and E002: the quota-side work is independent of the\nmetering schema (the Kueue quota model is GA in-platform per architecture\ncontext), so both roots run in parallel — this is the Tier 1 / Tier 2 split\nbelow.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Core reporting view: per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges computed from metering data. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | **Bundled** with HLR-1: CSV export and the stable API are presentation/retrieval modes of the same report data structure and API surface as HLR-1's reports. Splitting would create two epics building the same surface plus an artificial serialization edge between them; the P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002, RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Spans two components: E002 delivers the per-project quota reference and attribution semantics (Distributed Workloads side); E003 renders current-month consumption against quota in the admin view (Dashboard side). |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration; `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all Implementations. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n   Teams table, no Dependencies table (only a frontmatter `depends-on` link),\n   no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no non-functional requirements.\n   Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs; owner teams were\n   inferred from the architecture-context files (RHOAI Dashboard owns\n   Dashboard; Distributed Workloads owns Distributed Workloads). The\n   documentation epic's team is assumed to be RHOAI Dashboard (feature owner)\n   b