{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-95117506-3b19-4da7-b29d-3ffa958e1c57","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac02aa29f58f7e68","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9018-6afumvbx/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9018-E001.md","content":"# Validate GPU usage metering data schema for chargeback reporting\n\n## Description\n\nThe GPU usage metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990) is in flight and owns the\nper-project GPU-hour usage data schema, which is not final until that strategy\nlands. This investigation resolves the strategy's open question — the external\ntiming and shape of that schema — by tracking it as it stabilizes and\nvalidating that it can support administrator chargeback reporting. It produces\nthe reporting-side data contract that the reporting implementation builds\nagainst: which fields are consumed, the per-project aggregation rules, and how\nreporting accesses the metering data.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Map the finalized (or latest draft) metering data schema field-by-field\n  against the reporting needs: per-project GPU-hour totals, selectable time\n  ranges, and current-month windows.\n- Identify gaps where the schema cannot support reporting (project\n  attribution, time granularity, GPU-hour definition) and raise them to the\n  metering strategy owners, with each gap's disposition recorded.\n- Define the reporting-side data contract: consumed fields, per-project\n  aggregation rules, and the access path by which reporting reads the metering\n  data.\n- Record the agreed contract as the deliverable that downstream reporting work\n  builds against, including a go/no-go conclusion.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written schema-to-reporting mapping exists covering every field the\n  reporting needs (project identity, GPU-hours, timestamps), with each need\n  marked supported or gap.\n- Every gap has a recorded disposition: accepted limitation, or extension\n  request formally raised to the metering strategy owners.\n- The reporting data contract (consumed fields, aggregation rules, access\n  path) is documented and confirmed with the metering data owners.\n- A go/no-go conclusion is recorded: the finalized schema either supports\n  per-project GPU-hour reporting over selectable time ranges, or the required\n  extensions have been formally requested.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-1, HLR-2, and HLR-3 by resolving the strategy's open question on\n  metering data schema finalization; this investigation does not itself\n  implement any HLR.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- External timing risk: the schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands.\n  The investigation can start against drafts but cannot conclude before\n  finalization; the timing is outside this strategy's control.\n- The Dashboard owns no data plane (Kubernetes API only per architecture\n  context), so the contract must also settle how reporting accesses\n  time-series metering data, not just its schema.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":46679,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad32f63ef3803cbc","agent_id":"aa2535d4b490cc539","session_id":"ae63a1c3-308e-4096-a1af-f3bfa0f3fcf1","uuid":"a215d2b7-036d-47c6-9978-42abecaff71a","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:03:45.735Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9018"}
