{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-930879f00da9f790","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n10\t\n11\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Major. The strategy builds\n12\tadministrator-facing chargeback reporting on top of per-project GPU-hour\n13\tmetering data delivered by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990 strategy, whose data\n14\tschema is not final until that strategy lands.\n15\t\n16\t## Step 0 Triage\n17\t\n18\tNot triaged. The strategy is not below threshold: it affects two components\n19\t(Dashboard, Distributed Workloads), and its single open question — the\n20\tfinalization of the metering data schema owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 — is a\n21\tgenuine unknown whose answer changes what the reporting implementation builds\n22\tagainst. It is not documentation-only: HLR-1 through HLR-3 require code work.\n23\tFull decomposition (Steps 1-8) was executed.\n24\t\n25\t## Epic List\n26\t\n27\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n28\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Validate GPU usage metering data schema for chargeback reporting | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Per-project Kueue quota reference and GPU-hour attribution semantics | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Administrator GPU chargeback reporting in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Chargeback report semantics and export integration documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n33\t\n34\tPriorities: E001 is P0 because it gates the P0 reporting implementation\n35\t(HLR-1). E003 is P0 via HLR-1. E002 inherits P0 by priority inheritance: the\n36\treporting epic builds against its quota/semantics contract and transitively\n37\tenables HLR-1, even though E002's only direct HLR (HLR-3) is P1 — see Health\n38\tWarnings. E004 is a `docs-authoring` epic exempt from priority inheritance;\n39\tits P1 derives from the strategy frontmatter priority `Major` (Major → P1).\n40\t\n41\t## Dependency DAG\n42\t\n43\t```mermaid\n44\tgraph TD\n45\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: metering schema contract\"] --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard chargeback reporting\"]\n46\t    E002[\"E002 Quota reference + attribution semantics\"] --> E003\n47\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 Documentation\"]\n48\t    E003 --> E004\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tRoots (no dependencies): E001, E002. Critical path: E001 → E003 → E004\n52\t(length 3).\n53\t\n54\t## DAG Justification\n55\t\n56\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n57\t|------|------|-----------|\n58\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's open question — the metering data schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands — determines what the reporting implementation builds against. The investigation produces the reporting-side data contract; until it resolves, the reporting epic's approach is unknown. True gate: `rewrite` — if the finalized schema does not support per-project GPU-hour aggregation, the reporting implementation is rewritten against the actual schema (or extensions are requested to the metering strategy). |\n59\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact another epic builds against) | E002 delivers the validated per-project Kueue quota reference and GPU-hour attribution semantics; the Dashboard reporting view's code builds against that contract to render consumption-vs-quota comparison. Not a configuration reference — the quota mapping and semantics define the data shape the view consumes. |\n60\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | The documentation epic must be blocked by every Implementation epic; it also derives its report-semantics content from the attribution semantics E002 defines. |\n61\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes what was built — the reporting view, export, and API — so it lands last. |\n62\t\n63\tNo edges between E001 and E002: the quota-side work is independent of the\n64\tmetering schema (the Kueue quota model is GA in-platform per architecture\n65\tcontext), so both roots run in parallel — this is the Tier 1 / Tier 2 split\n66\tbelow.\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n69\t\n70\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n71\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n72\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Core reporting view: per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges computed from metering data. |\n73\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | **Bundled** with HLR-1: CSV export and the stable API are presentation/retrieval modes of the same report data structure and API surface as HLR-1's reports. Splitting would create two epics building the same surface plus an artificial serialization edge between them; the P1 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n74\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002, RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Spans two components: E002 delivers the per-project quota reference and attribution semantics (Distributed Workloads side); E003 renders current-month consumption against quota in the admin view (Dashboard side). |\n75\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration; `docs-authoring` epic blocked by all Implementations. |\n76\t\n77\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n78\t\n79\t## Health Warnings\n80\t\n81\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n82\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table (only a frontmatter `depends-on` link),\n83\t   no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no non-functional requirements.\n84\t   Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs; owner teams were\n85\t   inferred from the architecture-context files (RHOAI Dashboard owns\n86\t   Dashboard; Distributed Workloads owns Distributed Workloads). The\n87\t   documentation epic's team is assumed to be RHOAI Dashboard (feature owner)\n88\t   because the strategy names no documentation owner — verify before submit.\n89\t2. **External timing risk** (the strategy's open question): the metering data\n90\t   schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. This is outside this\n91\t   strategy's control; E001 converts it into a tracked gate with a `rewrite`\n92\t   failure impact, and Tier 2 work (E003, E004) is gated behind it.\n93\t3. **Priority inheritance note**: E002's only direct HLR is P1 (HLR-3), but it\n94\t   inherits P0 because the P0 reporting epic builds against its quota/\n95\t   semantics contract (an epic blocking all P0 work is implicitly P0). Flagged\n96\t   so reviewers do not read it as a priority inversion.\n97\t4. **Data-access path unspecified**: architecture context states the Dashboard\n98\t   owns no data plane and speaks the Kubernetes API only, while metering data\n99\t   is time-series usage data. The strategy does not say how reporting accesses\n100\t   it. This is folded into E001's contract validation (fields, aggregation\n101\t   rules, and access path) as cross-strategy coordination rather than a\n102\t   separate investigation — the answer is owned by the same external schema\n103\t   negotiation.\n104\t5. **\"Stable API\" commitment**: HLR-2's stable API for finance tooling implies\n105\t   a versioning/support commitment on the report retrieval surface; the\n106\t   implementing team should treat API stability as a deliverable property, not\n107\t   just an endpoint.\n108\t6. **Re-split trigger for E003**: the reporting epic bundles the report view,\n109\t   quota comparison, CSV export, and stable API — one logical change over one\n110\t   admin surface (splitting the view itself would build the same surface\n111\t   twice). It may exceed one sprint of effort. Re-split if implementation\n112\t   exceeds a sprint: the export/API work (HLR-2) may be split out only if a\n113\t   separately reviewable backend data-access surface emerges — never by\n114\t   splitting the admin view itself.\n115\t\n116\t## Tiered Delivery\n117\t\n118\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990 schema finalization) has uncertain\n119\ttiming, and the strategy can deliver partial value without it:\n120\t\n121\t- **Tier 1 — independent of the metering schema (start now)**:\n122\t  - E001 (risk-reduction): schema tracking and reporting-side contract\n123\t    validation can begin against RHAISTRAT-9990 drafts and concludes when the\n124\t    schema finalizes.\n125\t  - E002 (user-facing groundwork): the per-project quota reference and\n126\t    GPU-hour attribution semantics are derived from the in-platform Kueue\n127\t    quota model and do not wait on metering data.\n128\t- **Tier 2 — dependency-gated (blocked by E001 / the finalized schema)**:\n129\t  - E003: the reporting view, export, and API consume the metering data.\n130\t  - E004: documentation follows the implementations.\n131\t\n132\t## Component Mapping Notes\n133\t\n134\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n135\t`Dashboard` (E001, E003), `Distributed Workloads` (E002), `Documentation`\n136\t(E004). No non-obvious mappings. Both strategy components have\n137\tarchitecture-context files (in platform, Konflux onboarded), so no\n138\tonboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is generated. No Staff Engineer Input\n139\tsection is present in the strategy, so no overrides were logged.\n140\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a9b8e6182f098a6","agent_id":"a3e018191f58e0c6d","session_id":"ae63a1c3-308e-4096-a1af-f3bfa0f3fcf1","uuid":"19a5912c-cdd6-47e0-9dd1-7a6ee64b2381","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:09:36.403Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-930879f00da9f790","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config